cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use FlowMLLab, please cite this v1.12.0 software release and the relevant research-case articles."
title: "FlowMLLab: An Open-Source Framework for Reproducible CFD-to-Scientific-Machine-Learning Experiments"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Roohi
    given-names: Ehsan
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5739-3210"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Ehsan-Roohi/FlowMLLab"
url: "https://github.com/Ehsan-Roohi/FlowMLLab/releases/tag/v1.12.0"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  FlowMLLab integrates transparent continuum and particle solvers, case-wise
  data partitions, non-neural baselines, coordinate networks, POD-DeepONet,
  centered POD-Galerkin and POD-DEIM models, a D2Q9 lattice-Boltzmann
  cylinder-wake module, exact and learned gas-dynamics inverse benchmarks,
  physical diagnostics,
  machine-readable validation evidence, and release-quality assurance for
  reproducible scientific machine learning in fluid mechanics. Weeks 17-22
  add CPU laboratories on Q/K/V attention, a small character language model,
  sparse sensor reconstruction, autonomous wake prediction and controlled
  transfer comparisons, with separate student and instructor notebooks.
  Week 1.3 adds a CPU optimizer laboratory with matched comparisons and six
  student exercises, including a teaching implementation of SOAP and
  self-scaled Broyden. Three additional cavity notebooks cover all seven Ghia
  Reynolds numbers, a three-seed CPU profile-surrogate study, and retained
  D/W=5 CFD/PINN regional assessment. High-Re full-field qualification remains
  separate from centreline regression.
keywords:
  - computational fluid dynamics
  - scientific machine learning
  - neural operators
  - proper orthogonal decomposition
  - discrete empirical interpolation method
  - lattice Boltzmann method
  - cylinder wake
  - gas dynamics
  - compressible flow
  - direct simulation Monte Carlo
  - reproducibility
  - transformers
  - attention
  - language modeling
